w even if you can show us done  fraudulently that doesn't bother them  they can all just wave their court  decisions in the air  based on a you know 500 bribe they made  to the judge and  that's the order of magnitude of bribes  that they probably had to make in order  to get these kinds of decisions  through the chorus so you tell  one story and um  the way i like to sort of when i when i  read a non-fiction book about something  i'm not already familiar with i first  don't look up anything about it i just  read the book and then i'll look up well  what are the other  perspectives about it and after reading  your book and hearing your story  doing some research as well for this  interview i come across  allegations that you were not actually  the victim  but you actually were the perpetrator in  other words you use the systems  that you criticize and say that  victimized you to actually participate  in the corruption and in the fraud now  it's beyond my ability to adjudicate  these things in independently in the  context of this interview  you tell one story there are others who  tell another story  uh but what do you make of the fact that  there are these counter allegations that  have been made that say you are actually  the perpetrator here and not the victim  well i mean well first of all those  allegations were made  by the people who stole the money so um  and so so we have we have a uh uh i mean  and just so you know in in russia this  is what they do  they shoot down a plane going over  russia um  and they blame it on the on the the  people who were you know on the plane or  or i mean ev everything is the other  guys did it  and and uh in in our case uh  you know it's the whole thing is sort of  completely absurd from top to bottom the  money was stolen  um in so so our companies were stolen  and and all of these sort of  machinations were done  in to in in december like october  december of 2007  and we filed a criminal complaint with  with all the major russian law  enforcement  agencies after we learned about the  theft of our companies  and we put that onto the record  and three weeks after we filed our  complaint  the tax money was stolen the 230 million  dollars of taxes that we paid  and so what what criminals would would  file a complaint about their upcoming  crime  before they committed it i mean there  are so there are so many  like errors in their story they they  also blame the story  um that they say that that sergey  magnitsky my dead lawyer  um who was killed by the way for for  disclosing this  they say he stole the money and he did  it together with another dead guy  and the other dead guy died two two  months before the um the crime was  committed and so i mean  just you know these guys are like  keystone cops when it comes to  coming up with a conspiracy theory to  blame the other guy  so there um have been multiple red  notices  placed against you for people who don't  know what a red notice is a red notice  is  sort of a global interpol um  arrest warrant or arrest directive in a  sense  and there were ultimately interpol  decided as you tell in the book  not to enforce that red notice they  decided this is politically motivated  this is bogus and they decided not to  enforce it but there was a period of  time where  as far as you knew if you had an  interaction  with customs or border authorities  in any number of countries you could  have been arrested and then extradited  to russia and then  who knows what would have happened how  long was that period of time  where that was a concern for you where  you didn't know what would happen  well so the book um i finished writing  the book in 2014  and the first interpol um uh notice from  russia came in 2013.  the second one came in 2013 and since  the book was written  there have been eight interpol notices  for my arrest from russia and so when  you  say how long there's been numerous  periods of time  a week here two weeks there three weeks  um where  it where if i crossed a border i would  be arrested sent to russia  to my death and um i was arrested  in in may of 2018 i was traveling to  madrid  to meet with the spanish anti-corruption  prosecutor to share information  about russian uh money laundering  connected to the murder of my lawyer  sergey magnitsky  and as i was walking out of my hotel  room before my meeting with  with the spanish prosecutor two  spanish national policemen arrested me  on a russian interpol  warrant and it was only  because i was allowed to pack my bags in  my hotel room  before they took me away that i tweeted  out that i was being arrested  and then all hell broke loose with  journalists and  activists and everybody else calling  interpol  calling the spanish government calling  everybody else so that by the time i got  to the police station i was released but  um  uh this kind of stuff is pretty scary  and it continues to plague me up until  today the russians are after me  they've issued uh they've found me  guilty twice  in their courts in absentia and have  sent me since me 18 years in prison  um i'd be killed if i went back there no  question  yeah you you know what's interesting is  you you talk in the book  about how something that vladimir putin  will  do sometimes when he's asked about  individuals that are his adversaries and  this this happened with you he won't say  the person's name and so  there was a event you described in the  book where vladimir putin was asked  about you  and he referred to you i think as like  the gentleman you're referring to  when i was watching recently uh the  biden  putin press conference that took place  after their meeting  and putin was asked about navalny by a  journalist  it was almost verbatim what was said to  you putin  didn't say his name and said something  like  the person you're referring to  i believe has committed crimes in our  country which was i mean  that's essentially the same language you  describe is it not well  he 